                    <p class="guide-caption">While the uplink is down, the Collector keeps accepting data and writes each batch to the queue on disk. A restart doesn't empty it. When the link returns, the backlog drains and the queue falls back to zero.</p>

                    <h3 id="pipeline" class="scroll-mt-24">The pipeline, stage by stage</h3>
                    <p>
                        Inside the Collector, every signal passes through the same stages in this order. Each one is a component you name in the config in <a href="#config">section 03</a>.
                    </p>
                    <ol class="guide-steps">
                        {pipeline.map((p, index) => (
                            <li style={`--c: ${p.concept ? concepts[p.concept].color : neutral}`}>
                                <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                                <div><h4><code>{p.component}</code></h4><p>{p.body}</p></div>
                            </li>
                        ))}
                    </ol>

                    <h3 id="gateways" class="scroll-mt-24">Regional gateways</h3>
                    <p>
                        Large fleets sometimes add a gateway tier: one Collector per region that every site in the region exports to. It is worth running when you need a single egress point per region, or want the backend's ingest key kept off site hardware. Without one of those reasons, export from each site directly. A gateway is one more machine that can be down, which is why it is dashed in the diagram.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">Edge observability tools</h3>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it is</th><th>License</th><th>At the edge</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/collector/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry Collector</a></td><td>Receives, processes and exports traces, metrics and logs</td><td>Open source (Apache 2.0)</td><td>Exporter queue on disk (<code>file_storage</code>, contrib build) and a memory ceiling (<code>memory_limiter</code>). Used in this guide.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><a href="https://docs.fluentbit.io/manual/administration/buffering-and-storage" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fluent Bit</a></td><td>Lightweight forwarder for logs, metrics and traces, a CNCF graduated project</td><td>Open source (Apache 2.0)</td><td>Buffers to the filesystem, with a size cap per output that drops the oldest data first</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><a href="https://vector.dev/docs/architecture/buffering-model/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vector</a></td><td>Observability data pipeline, maintained by Datadog</td><td>Open source (MPL 2.0)</td><td>Disk buffers that survive restarts and crashes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><a href="https://docs.px.dev/installing-pixie/requirements/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pixie</a></td><td>Kubernetes observability from eBPF probes, no code changes, a CNCF sandbox project</td><td>Open source (Apache 2.0)</td><td>Needs Kubernetes 1.21+, Linux kernel 4.14+ and at least 1 GiB of memory per node</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Backend</td><td>Stores and queries what every site sends: Maple or any other OTLP backend</td><td>Varies</td><td>Receives OTLP over HTTP; the site's Collector does the buffering</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p class="guide-caption">Facts from each project's own documentation, last verified October 31, 2026.</p>
                </section>

                <section id="config" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>03</span> · Config</p>
                    <h2>The edge Collector config</h2>
                    <p>
                        This config runs on the site's gateway machine with <a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-releases/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry Collector Contrib</a> (<code>otelcol-contrib</code>), the distribution that includes the file storage, filter and sampling components. It exports to Maple. For another OTLP/HTTP backend, change the endpoint and the header.
                    </p>

                    <div class="code-file">
                        <div class="code-file__head">
                            <p class="code-file__name">edge-site.yaml</p>
                            <div class="code-file__legend">
                                {Object.values(concepts).map((c) => <span class="type-badge" style={`--type: ${c.color}`}>{c.name}</span>)}
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <pre><code set:html={configHtml} /></pre>
                    </div>

                    <h3 id="buffering" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="type-dot" style={`--type: ${concepts.buffer.color}`}></span>Buffer while offline</h3>
                    <p>
                        How long a site can stay offline without losing data comes down to the exporter's queue and retry settings:
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {queueSettings.map(([setting, value, effect]) => (
                                    <tr><td><code>{setting}</code></td><td><code>{value}</code></td><td>{effect}</td></tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Size the queue from your own numbers: the site's telemetry per hour, times the longest outage you want to ride out, on a disk with room for it. Even with the queue, data is lost when:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>A crash loses what the <code>batch</code> processor holds in memory, up to its <code>timeout</code> (10 seconds here), before it reaches the queue.</li>
                        <li>A full queue drops new data, counted in <code>otelcol_exporter_enqueue_failed_spans</code>, <code>_metric_points</code> and <code>_log_records</code>.</li>
                        <li>A non-retryable error drops the batch. The exporter retries <code>429</code> and <code>503</code>; most other <code>4xx</code> responses, such as a bad ingest key, are final.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>
                        Watch the backlog through the Collector's own metric <code>otelcol_exporter_queue_size</code>, the number of batches waiting, plotted in the diagram above. A queue that stays above zero means the site is offline or its uplink can't keep up.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="volume" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="type-dot" style={`--type: ${concepts.reduce.color}`}></span>Cut volume before the uplink</h3>
                    <p>
                        Data dropped at the site never crosses the uplink and is never stored. The config cuts volume at these points:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>filter/noise</code> removes data nobody queries: health checks and debug logs. It is exact and cheap, so it runs before sampling.</li>
                        <li><code>probabilistic_sampler</code> in <code>proportional</code> mode keeps 25% of traces and writes the sampling rate into each span's <code>tracestate</code> (<code>ot=th:c</code> for 25%). A backend that reads it can scale counts back up. Maple does, so throughput and error rate still estimate the full traffic (<a href="/docs/concepts/sampling-throughput">sampling and throughput</a>).</li>
                        <li><code>compression: gzip</code> on the exporter. Telemetry is repetitive and compresses well.</li>
                        <li><code>collection_interval: 60s</code> on host metrics sends half the data points of a 30-second interval.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>
                        Tail sampling decides after a trace has finished, so it can keep every error and every slow request plus a small share of the rest. It needs all spans of a trace in one Collector and holds traces in memory for <code>decision_wait</code>, which costs RAM a small gateway may not have. For a site whose traces stay local, add this processor in place of <code>probabilistic_sampler</code>:
                    </p>
                    <div class="code-file">
                        <div class="code-file__head"><p class="code-file__name">tail_sampling (optional)</p></div>
                        <pre><code set:html={`<span class="cfg-part" style="--c: ${concepts.reduce.color}">${highlight(tailSamplingConfig, "yaml")}</span>`} /></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        The tail sampler writes no rate into <code>tracestate</code>, so a backend counts each kept span once and request counts from these traces lean toward errors and slow requests. For exact request metrics, count every span before sampling with the Collector's span metrics connector, as the <a href="/docs/concepts/sampling-throughput#exact-counts-with-spanmetrics">SpanMetrics section</a> of Maple's sampling docs shows.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="site-identity" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="type-dot" style={`--type: ${concepts.identify.color}`}></span>Tag every signal with its site</h3>
                    <p>
                        A <strong>resource attribute</strong> describes what produced a piece of telemetry, such as the service or the host, and is attached to every span, metric and log from it. OpenTelemetry has no standard attribute for a physical site, so define your own and set it in one place: the <code>resource/site</code> processor stamps it on everything the site's Collector forwards.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Example</th><th>Set by</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {siteAttributes.map(([attribute, example, source]) => (
                                    <tr><td><code>{attribute}</code></td><td><code>{example}</code></td><td>{source}</td></tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Prefix custom attributes with your company's name (<code>acme.</code> here) so they can't collide with a future OpenTelemetry convention. The site keys use <code>upsert</code>, which overwrites, so an application with a stale setting can't report itself as another site. The environment uses <code>insert</code>, which only fills it in when the application left it empty.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="run" class="scroll-mt-24">Run and test it</h3>
                    <p>
                        Save the config as <code>edge-site.yaml</code>, validate it, and start the Collector with the site's identity and your ingest key in the environment:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(runCommand, "shell")} /></pre>

                    <div class="guide-tested">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Tested on otelcol-contrib 0.163.1</p>
                        <p>
                            Run in Docker with the container's network cut, the Collector accepted spans, logs and host metrics and queued them while the exporter retried. After a <code>SIGKILL</code> and a restart on the same volume with the network back, the queued data was delivered. The filter dropped the <code>/healthz</code> span and the debug log, and kept a log with no severity set.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="guide-notes">
                        {runNotes.map(([title, body]) => (
                            <div><h4>{title}</h4><p set:html={body} /></div>
                        ))}
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="edge-kubernetes" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>04</span> · Edge Kubernetes</p>
                    <h2>Edge Kubernetes and containers</h2>
                    <p>
                        Many edge sites run a small Kubernetes distribution such as k3s on one to three nodes. Container observability there covers the same signals as in a cloud cluster: node and pod CPU and memory against requests and limits, pod phases and node conditions, and the traces of the workloads inside.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Maple's <code>maple-k8s-infra</code> Helm chart works on k3s, kind and k0s without cert-manager. It runs a Collector on every node for host, kubelet and pod metrics, with OTLP receivers on <code>:4317</code> and <code>:4318</code> for apps in the cluster, and one more for cluster-wide metrics. Install it at each site with the site as the cluster name:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(helmCommand, "shell")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        The chart sets <code>k8s.cluster.name</code> from <code>global.clusterName</code>, which is the site identity for these sites. Its exporter keeps the Collector's default queue in memory with a five-minute retry, which covers short drops; a long outage or a pod restart loses the backlog. At a site that goes offline for hours, send application telemetry through a standalone Collector with the disk queue from <a href="#config">section 03</a>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        On a single box that runs Docker without Kubernetes, the <a href="/docs/infrastructure/docker">Docker agent</a> collects per-container metrics and logs, and the <a href="/docs/infrastructure/hosts">Hosts</a> Collector config covers the machine itself.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="cloudflare-workers" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>05</span> · Serverless edge</p>
                    <h2>Cloudflare Workers and other edge functions</h2>
                    <p>
                        An edge function has no host to run a Collector on and no disk to buffer to. A Worker that exports from its own code with an OpenTelemetry SDK must pass the final flush to <code>ctx.waitUntil()</code>, which keeps the runtime alive until the export finishes. Otherwise the last spans can be lost when the request ends.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Cloudflare Workers can also record and export telemetry with no SDK. <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/observability/traces/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Workers tracing</a> creates spans for handler invocations, outbound <code>fetch</code> calls, binding calls such as KV and R2, and Durable Object RPC. <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/observability/opentelemetry-export/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry export</a> sends those traces and the Worker's logs over OTLP to destinations you create in the Cloudflare dashboard. A Worker opts in through its Wrangler config:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(wranglerConfig, "jsonc")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>head_sampling_rate</code> is the share of invocations recorded, from 0 to 1, default 1. <code>persist: false</code> sends data only to the destinations, without also storing it in Cloudflare. As of October 2026:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Automatic tracing is in beta, and span and attribute names can change.</li>
                        <li>Metrics are not part of the export. Request counts, errors and CPU time come from Cloudflare's analytics API.</li>
                        <li>Trace context is not passed to services outside Cloudflare, so a backend the Worker calls starts its own trace.</li>
                        <li>Some spans show 0 ms. As a Spectre mitigation, the runtime only advances its clock on I/O, so CPU work between two I/O calls has no duration.</li>
                        <li>Each span carries <code>cloudflare.colo</code>, the data center that ran it: the location identity at the serverless edge.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section id="in-maple" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>06</span> · In Maple</p>
                    <h2>Edge observability in Maple</h2>
                    <p>
                        Maple receives OTLP over HTTP, so both kinds of edge send to the same endpoint: <code>https://ingest.maple.dev</code>, or <code>https://ingest.eu.maple.dev</code> for EU organizations. Each needs a private ingest key (<code>maple_sk_…</code>) from <strong>Settings → Ingestion</strong>.
                    </p>

                    <LanguageTabs label="Kind of edge" group="maple-guide-edge-kind" tabs={[{ id: "edge-sites", label: "Edge sites" }, { id: "workers", label: "Cloudflare Workers" }]}>
                        <LanguageTab id="edge-sites">
                            <ol class="guide-steps">
                                <li>
                                    <span>01</span>
                                    <div><h4>Start the Collector at a site</h4><p>Run the config from section 03 with the site's <code>SITE_ID</code> and your key. The gateway appears under <strong>Infrastructure → Hosts</strong> within about a minute, keyed on <code>host.name</code>, with CPU, memory and disk.</p>
                                        <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/edge-observability-01-hosts.webp" data-zoomable alt="The Hosts page row for the edge gateway gw-0142: Linux, arm64, CPU, memory and disk usage bars, 15-minute load and last-seen time." width="2340" height="324" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <span>02</span>
                                    <div><h4>Filter by site</h4><p>In <strong>Explore → Traces</strong>, click <strong>Advanced Filter</strong> and enter <code>resource.acme.site.id = "store-0142"</code> to see one site's traces. Every span's <strong>Resource Attributes</strong> list the site ID and region.</p>
                                        <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/edge-observability-02-site-filter.webp" data-zoomable alt="The traces list with the advanced filter resource.acme.site.id = &quot;store-0142&quot; applied: every trace comes from the site's pos-api service." width="1814" height="1250" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <span>03</span>
                                    <div><h4>Edge Kubernetes sites</h4><p>Sites installed with the Helm chart carry the site as the cluster name: filter with <code>resource.k8s.cluster.name = "store-0142"</code>.</p></div>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </LanguageTab>
                        <LanguageTab id="workers">
                            <ol class="guide-steps">
                                <li>
                                    <span>01</span>
                                    <div><h4>Create two destinations</h4><p>In Cloudflare, open <strong>Workers &amp; Pages → Observability → Destinations</strong>. Add a Traces destination named <code>maple-traces</code> for <code>https://ingest.maple.dev/v1/traces</code> and a Logs destination named <code>maple-logs</code> for <code>/v1/logs</code>, each with an <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header carrying the key. OTLP export needs the Workers Paid plan.</p></div>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <span>02</span>
                                    <div><h4>Reference them and deploy</h4><p>Add the <code>observability</code> block from section 05 to the Worker's Wrangler config and run <code>wrangler deploy</code>. Each request becomes a trace, and a span's <strong>Cloudflare Worker</strong> panel shows the script, CPU and wall time, and edge location.</p></div>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <span>03</span>
                                    <div><h4>Add Worker metrics</h4><p>Connect <strong>Integrations → Cloudflare</strong> with OAuth. Maple polls Workers invocations, errors and CPU time every 5 minutes, and a traced Worker gets a <strong>Cloudflare edge</strong> section on the service map.</p></div>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </LanguageTab>
                    </LanguageTabs>

                    <div class="guide-maple">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Set it up in Maple</p>
                        <h3>Step-by-step docs for each edge</h3>
                        <p>Prerequisites, verification and troubleshooting for each path, including EU endpoints.</p>
